@block introduction | start | 1.1 | One turn can contain more than one answer
@prose An equation such as sin(theta)=1/2 asks for angles theta, not for the number 1/2 itself. This reading works in radians and finds every permitted angle in one turn: from 0 up to, but not including, 2pi. The six exercises move from reading that interval to checking a worked set, choosing an algebra move, explaining a reflection, repairing an endpoint error, and solving a fresh case.
@prose A solution set is the collection of every angle that makes the original equation true and also lies in its stated interval. A list with one correct angle can still be incomplete.
@endblock

@block definition | terms | 1.2 | Angles, radians, and height
@prose A radian is an angle unit. Pi, written pi, is the exact constant for a half-turn, so 2pi radians is one complete turn. Theta is the variable angle measured in radians. In this lesson theta is real: it may be any real-number angle before an interval restricts it.
@display unit_circle_height
\sin(\theta)=\text{the vertical coordinate, or height, of the point on the unit circle at angle }\theta
@prose Thus sine is positive above the horizontal axis, zero on that axis, and negative below it. For the special heights in this pilot, sin(pi/6)=1/2, sin(5pi/6)=1/2, sin(7pi/6)=-1/2, sin(11pi/6)=-1/2, sin(0)=sin(pi)=0, and sin(pi/2)=1.
@endblock

@block definition | interval | 1.3 | Read both endpoints before solving
@prose The notation 0<=theta<2pi is an interval condition. The left inequality includes theta=0 because it uses <=. The right inequality excludes theta=2pi because it uses <. Although 0 and 2pi point in the same direction, this interval records that direction once, at 0.
@display interval_rule
0\le\theta<2\pi
@prose An integer parameter is a whole number k: it may be negative, zero, or positive. It describes repeated turns. After an interval is imposed, keep only the integer values that make the corresponding angle meet both endpoint conditions.
@endblock

@block proposition | rule | 1.4 | Find all matching heights, then filter
@prose For a non-top special height, first locate each point on one unit-circle turn with that height. Reflection across the vertical axis sends an angle alpha to pi-alpha and preserves its vertical coordinate, so sin(pi-alpha)=sin(alpha). Then repeat each point by complete turns, written 2kpi, where k is an integer. Finally intersect those repeated angles with the stated interval.
@display reflection_rule
\sin(\pi-\alpha)=\sin(\alpha)
@prose For sin(theta)=1/2, the two branches are theta=pi/6+2kpi and theta=5pi/6+2kpi. For sin(theta)=-1/2, they are theta=7pi/6+2kpi and theta=11pi/6+2kpi. Do not call one principal inverse value the full solution set: it supplies at most one starting angle.
@help proof
@prose On the unit circle, a horizontal line at a height strictly between -1 and 1 meets the circle at the two points with that height. For the listed special heights, those points give the two displayed angles in a single turn. Sine repeats after 2pi, so adding 2kpi lists the matching points on every other turn. There are no third intersections with that horizontal line, so there are no further branches. At height 1 the horizontal line touches only the top point, so its reflected description is the same point and must be deduplicated.
@endblock

@block example | condition | 1.5 | Isolate sine without changing the equation
@prose Algebra can expose the height to locate. For example, begin with 2sin(theta)-1=0. Add 1 to both complete sides, then divide both complete sides by the nonzero number 2. Adding 1 is reversed by subtracting 1, and dividing by 2 is reversed by multiplying by 2, so these moves preserve exactly the solutions.
@display isolate_sine
2\sin(\theta)-1=0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad2\sin(\theta)=1\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\sin(\theta)=\frac{1}{2}
@prose The restriction that 2 is nonzero matters: division by zero is undefined and cannot undo a multiplication. Once sine is isolated, use the unit-circle branches and the interval; algebra alone does not decide how many angles have that height.
@endblock

@block example | worked | 1.6 | Worked example: the top point occurs once
@prose Solve the separate example sin(theta)=1 with 0<=theta<2pi. Height 1 is the top of the unit circle, so the only point in one turn is theta=pi/2. A reflected angle would be pi-pi/2=pi/2, the same angle rather than a second answer.
@help hint
@prose Ask which unit-circle point has vertical coordinate 1. Then check whether adding or subtracting one whole turn can remain in the stated interval.
@help answer
@display worked_answer
\theta\in\left\{\frac{\pi}{2}\right\}
@help solution
@prose The top point has angle pi/2, so sin(pi/2)=1. Its periodic copies are pi/2+2kpi for integer k. The interval test is 0<=1/2+2k<2 when angles are divided by pi. Only k=0 works: k=-1 is below 0 and k=1 is at least 2. Therefore theta=pi/2 is the one permitted solution.
@display worked_check
\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)=1,\qquad0\le\frac{\pi}{2}<2\pi
@endblock

@block example | errors | 1.7 | Repair the first line that violates an endpoint
@prose For sin(theta)=0, the all-real-angle pattern is theta=kpi. In 0<=theta<2pi, the allowed integer values are k=0 and k=1. The value k=2 would produce the excluded endpoint 2pi. If a student's first bad line keeps k=2, later substitution can be arithmetically faithful yet still inherit that first interval error.
@display zero_error
\text{Deliberately incorrect: }k\in\{0,1,2\}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\theta\in\{0,\pi,2\pi\}
@prose Check errors in order: first the general branch, then the endpoint filter, then the substituted set. Repairing the earliest wrong line keeps zero and removes 2pi.
@endblock

@block exercise | practice | 1.8 | Use the reading, then decide
@prose Each exercise keeps the original given visible. A wrong selection does not replace the cyan working: read its specific correction, compare it with the interval or unit-circle condition, and choose again. The final exercise gives a new equation and asks for the complete set without supplying a quadrant or an isolation step. Return to this reading if you need the definitions or the completed example.
@endblock

@block summary | summary | 1.9 | A complete answer has three checks
@prose State radians, isolate sine when necessary, locate every unit-circle point with the required height, repeat by 2kpi, and filter with both interval endpoints. Check each listed angle in the original equation. Completeness also needs a reason: the relevant horizontal line has only the stated unit-circle intersections, and periodicity accounts for their other turns.
@prose These original explanations use the exact special-angle cases 0, 1/2, -1/2, and 1 only. Other sine levels, inverse-trigonometric notation, degrees, identities, and arbitrary trigonometric equations are later families rather than promises of this pilot.
@endblock
